  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Gusty west winds Friday afternoon creating near-critical fire\n  weather conditions across Southwest Montana, choppy conditions\n  at area lakes, and hazardous conditions for empty, high-profile\n  vehicles on I-90 Deer Lodge to Butte, and I-15 south of Butte.\n\n- Strong Northeast winds develop Saturday causing choppy waves\n  that could pose a danger to docks and boats on the southwestern\n  portion of Flathead Lake. Isolated power outages are possible\n  across northwest Montana through Saturday night. \n\n- The first Winter Storm is expected to bring snow, cold\n  temperatures and gusty northeast winds to the mountains and high\n  valleys from west- central to southwest and central Montana late\n  Saturday through Sunday and Sunday night. This could highly \n  impact backcountry recreation, hunters and travel.\n\nToday and tomorrow will be the last summer-like days for us for a\nwhile. On Friday, a relatively dry cold front will move across the\nregion. The westerly wind gradient will tighten and southwest\nwinds of 15 to 25 mph will bring a 1 to 2 foot chop on Flathead\nLake. Stronger winds of 20 to 30 mph with gusts to 45 mph are\npossible from Lemhi County to southwest Montana. Besides the\ndangers of cross-winds on north-south highways, the combination of\ndry and windy conditions will create near-critical fire weather\nconditions across southwest Montana. \n\nOn Saturday, as a low pressure system moves onshore over \nWashington and Oregon, a northeast wind gradient will develop \nacross much of the region. At the same time, a cold, sub- \nfreezing airmass will push south out of Alberta Canada which will\nenhance the northeast winds even more. Flathead Lake will again\nexperience choppy conditions, but from the opposite direction\n(from the northeast) by Saturday afternoon. Northeast winds of 15\nto 25 mph with gusts to 35 mph could pose a danger to docks and\nboats from Yellow Bay south and west towards the Narrows and Big\nArm. The lake is at 2,892.52 feet which is 0.48 feet below full\npool. \n\nMoisture wrapping around the trof over Oregon will overrun the\ncold air that will be pushing southwards across western Montana\nSaturday night. Snow levels will be falling to valley floors along\nand west of the Divide. The cold air will start to lose steam as\nit banks up against the Idaho/Montana border, so snow levels in\ncentral Idaho will be higher generally 3000 to 4000 feet. \nTemperatures falling into the 20s at MacDonald Pass, combined with\ngusty northeast winds and snow will be enough to overcome the \nwarm ground and could make travel conditions slick. Other \nlocations including Georgetown Lake, Butte, and Homestake Pass \ncould also experience winter-like conditions. The highest \nprobabilities for 3 inches of snow or greater exist in these \nareas. Depending on how far north the moisture travels, even rain \ncould changeover to snow for places like Missoula but may be \nminimal impact come Sunday morning except for the chill in the \nair! This is a very rare circumstance, but moisture from the \nremnants of Hurricane Rachel will track northeastwards from \nCalifornia and Nevada towards the Yellowstone and Wyoming area. \nWe'll be just on the edge of further moisture and lingering snow \nlater Sunday into Sunday night...i.e. Butte and Homestake Pass on \nI-90 may hold on to snow Sunday night!\n\nAs the weather system pulls away on Monday, high temperatures \nwill be 5 to 8 degrees below average.",
